        "prompt": {
          "raw": "You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\nMy consumer throws on event types it doesn't handle, and the platform keeps redelivering. What should it do?",
          "label": "evals/akka-sdk-consumers/prompt-baseline.txt: You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\n{{request}}",
          "config": {}
        },
        "promptId": "d03521c654409d4475c887a69078a2496ec5cd2ac7f26ff99487272af8c1f470",
        "promptIdx": 0,
        "provider": {
          "id": "exec:../_provider/claude.sh",
          "label": "sonnet-baseline"
        },
        "response": {
          "output": "Return `effects().ignore()` for event types you don't care about. Only real processing failures should throw, because an exception triggers redelivery.\n\n```java\npublic Effect onEvent(CounterEvent event) {\n  return switch (event) {\n    case CounterEvent.ValueIncreased v -> { /* handle */ yield effects().done(); }\n    default                            -> effects().ignore();   // unhandled: skip, don't throw\n  };\n}\n```\n\n- **Sealed event types:** A `default ->` branch on a sealed hierarchy hides new event types, so a type you later add is skipped without any compiler warning. If you want a warning, list the ignored cases explicitly and leave out `default`.\n- **Topic consumers:** The handler is chosen by `ce-type`. Make the same call for message types you don't handle: return `ignore()`.\n- **Duplicates:** Delivery is at-least-once, and the SDK doesn't dedup. Keep the handlers you do write idempotent."
        },
        "score": 1,
        "success": true,
