        "prompt": {
          "raw": "You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\nI need to observe every intermediate change of a key-value entity via a consumer. Workable?",
          "label": "evals/akka-sdk-consumers/prompt-baseline.txt: You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\n{{request}}",
          "config": {}
        },
        "promptId": "d03521c654409d4475c887a69078a2496ec5cd2ac7f26ff99487272af8c1f470",
        "promptIdx": 0,
        "provider": {
          "id": "exec:../_provider/claude.sh",
          "label": "sonnet-baseline"
        },
        "response": {
          "output": "No, that isn't workable. A consumer on a key-value entity (`@Consume.FromKeyValueEntity`) only gets the most recent state. If the entity updates several times quickly, the consumer can skip the intermediate states. Delivery is also at-least-once, so you will see duplicates as well as gaps.\n\nIf you need every change, you have two options:\n\n- **Use an event-sourced entity.** A consumer with `@Consume.FromEventSourcedEntity` sees every event in order, and each state change is a journaled event. This is the right fit.\n- **Keep the key-value entity and emit the changes yourself.** Put a change log in the state, for example a version counter plus a list of recent transitions, and have the consumer read it. It's fragile, because you reimplement an event journal by hand and the log still has to survive the consumer skipping states.\n\nI recommend converting to an event-sourced entity. Whichever you pick, make the consumer idempotent, for example by tracking sequence numbers, because redelivery is expected."
        },
        "score": 1,
        "success": true,
